  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 330 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Becoming breezy today in the wake of a cold front.\nMainly dry but an isolated shower is possible in the far SE through\nmid aftn.\n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates yesterday's upper level trough\nnow well off the coast, with the next trough currently situated\nacross Quebec, and stretching SW across lower Ontario and into \nthe upper OH Valley. At the surface, a pre-frontal trough is \nalready over the region, with a cold front near the spine of the\nAppalachians. Warm and muggy conditions by mid-September standards\nprevail with most areas still seeing dew pts of 70F+, but this \nwill be the last morning for the next several days with these \nconditions. Patchy ground fog has developed, but VSBYs are \ngenerally 1-3 miles so dense fog is not a concern. With the \nfront continuing to push SE and closer to the area over the next\nfew hrs, expect the fog to diminish near sunrise. \n\nFor today, the upper level trough dives SE and passes across the\nDelmarva this aftn, sliding offshore by early evening. With an \nupper level ridge slowly expanding NE from the lower MS Valley,\nthe flow aloft becomes NW and will allow the cold front to\nrapidly drop across the local area later this morning. Expect\nsome increase in clouds as this occurs, with a few hrs of BKN \ncloud cover possible across the SE into the early-mid aftn\nbefore skies become mainly clear. Will maintain a slight chance\nPoP this aftn across NE NC where the dew pts remain higher in\nthe wake of the front, but storms are not expected. Otherwise, \nmuch drier air surging in from the north, along with pressure\nrises will allow for a breezy N to NE win later this morning\nthrough most of the afternoon, with gusts to 20-25 mph inland\nand ~30 mph near the coast. Afternoon dew pts look to mix out\ninto the 50s across the north and 60s in the south.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry with seasonable temperatures and significantly\nlower humidity tonight through Thursday morning. Temperatures \nwarm back above normal late in the week an upper ridge builds \nback into the region. Next chance for rain late Fri-Sat.\n\nAs high pressure builds in behind the front tonight, much cooler\nand pleasant conditions are expected with lows mostly in the \nmid-upper 50s to lower 60s, but ranging from as cool as near 50F\nin the far NW to the upper 60s/near 70F at the immediate coast\nin SE VA and NC OBX with the NE winds off the water. The upper \nlevel ridge becomes nearly stationary across the TN Valley Tue- \nWed as a weak upper level trough develops off the SE Coast. This\npattern will allow strong surface high pressure (~1030 mb), to \nset up from off the New England coast SW into the Carolina \ncoastal plain through midweek. Highs will stay mostly in the \nlow-mid 80s through Wed with lows in the 50s to lower 60s \n(locally warmer at the immediate coast). The models now mostly \nhave the upper trough to our S/SE potentially amplifying late \nin the week which would retrograde the ridge back towards the \nlower MS Valley. Overall, a decent amount of uncertainty as to \nhow this evolves, but the forecast will continue to show \ntemperatures climbing back to above normal for mid-September, \nwith some low-end rain chances late Fri-Sat, and through Sunday.",
